# Pirate Sea Race

A browser multiplayer pirate treasure race. The game is specified in [`docs/spec.md`](docs/spec.md) (the text of `docs/spec.docx`); [`docs/architecture.md`](docs/architecture.md) maps this repository onto it.

A match can be played and won end to end (§2, §5). Browsers create or join a private match by a six-character code and gather in its lobby, which lists the players; the host starts once two or more are in (late arrivals are refused). A 20-second countdown follows while the arena loads, then player control starts with 45 seconds of departure protection and the 35:00 timer counting down. The first player to hold every rubbing type is told the exact route to the final island (a compass, an arrow and a mark on the minimap), and everyone learns its sector in a banner; minute 20 and minute 28 bring more reveals. Stop inside the treasure circle holding every type and hold E for ten seconds to win: moving, letting go, losing a type, disconnecting or being grappled resets the claim, and any rival in range can grapple you. The match ends with the winner, or with no winner at the deadline, and every browser shows the results: each player's rubbings, thefts, losses, duels, gold, caches and islands.

Ships sail placeholder hulls around the slice sea: a compact map with four small islands with docks, reefs and shoal walls that funnel every route between islands through one central crossing, and eight outer-sea spawn slots. Up (or W) sails ahead, down (S) brakes and then reverses, left and right (A, D) turn, and E docks a ship beside a dock, at any speed. The authoritative server decides every position and sends each browser only the ships within its sight radius; the minimap's fog clears where the player has sailed.

Fights happen at sea (§6): J grapples the nearest ship ahead within six tiles. The attacker sees its aim, the target a warning with the side it comes from, and it lands after a one-second windup unless the target sails out of range or out of the aim (a miss costs six seconds). A grapple that lands stops both ships; after a three-second countdown the two fight the online duel inside the match, in the same page's arena, while everyone else in sight sees crossed swords and the elapsed time where they fight. The winner sails on with eight seconds of protection; the loser respawns at a dock 15 seconds after the fight, with 20 seconds of protection; a draw returns both to separate nearby points.

The race is for rubbings (§4; the slice plays Sun and Moon, two sealed sources on each island). Each island's rubbings are locked behind its ruin (§3): moored at its dock, press R to read the clue (six carved tablets whose hints give three symbols in order, or a 4 × 4 grid to walk from start to goal) and enter the answer with the keys or clicks. The first solver unlocks the island for everyone, and every solver earns 40 gold once; a wrong answer locks you out for three seconds. Then keep holding E: after six seconds you collect its rubbing of a type you lack (the HUD shows what the island holds, only while you are moored there, and the hold's progress). Win a duel at sea and, within five seconds, press 1 or 2 (or click) to steal one of the loser's types you lack, with 20 % of their gold (the rarest type is taken if you do not choose; a draw moves nothing). The purse beside the game shows your gold, your rubbings and what the last duel took or gave, and whether the winner chose it or the default took it. A player who drops keeps their seat, ship and rubbings for 60 seconds (a fighter away from a duel for ten seconds forfeits it); one who never returns, or quits, leaves a recovery cache at a dock, a chest others see when in sight. Moor there and hold E to claim one rubbing of a type you lack.

Fruit powers join the race (§9). Four fruit pedestals, two Ember and two Smoke, stand at island docks; a pedestal in sight shows whether it still holds its fruit. Moored at a pedestal's dock, press F to read its kit: the two specials and what they're good for. Hold F for three seconds to eat it. Letting go, casting off, a grapple or a disconnect stops the meal, and if two captains finish together the fruit goes to one of them. The kit replaces your two specials (U, I) in every duel at sea for the rest of the match, even after a defeat. A duel shows both fighters' kits. The arena draws Ember's flame bolts and Smoke's smoke screens, and a fighter inside a screen still shows a startup flash, its position and its health.

Every captain starts with a rumor (§3): an island where a rubbing type they lack is still sealed, or a sector with a recovery cache holding one. Press M for the journal: your rumors with why you got them (start, fresh, targeted, replacement, sighting), stale ones greyed out with what replaced them, the islands you have seen and whether their ruin is open, your types and your kit. A rumor whose target is emptied goes stale and is replaced; losing a rubbing in a duel gives a free fresh lead (§11); when every copy of a type is carried, the lead is a coarse carrier sighting, 30 to 60 seconds old. Moored at any dock, the journal sells a targeted rumor about a type you lack for 25 gold (Tab to its button, Enter), charged only when a lead is delivered. The sea shows its sector borders while the journal is open or a sector rumor is active.

The standalone duel (the combat spike, §18 stage 1) runs beside it: "Create duel" makes a 1v1 duel whose code the second browser enters under "Join duel", and "Practice" fights a server-side dummy (idle or guarding). After a three-second countdown both browsers predict the fight with the shared duel simulation and roll back to the server's inputs (`docs/duel.md`). Keys (§8): WASD or the arrows move (hold back to guard), J light, K heavy, U and I specials, Shift dash, J and K together throw. The HUD shows both health bars, stamina, the round clock, the connection line (round trip, input delay, corrections) and the result; "Show hitboxes" draws hurtboxes and active hitboxes.

## Requirements

Node 24 and npm 11. The browser tests use Playwright's Chromium for `@playwright/test` 1.63.0, which must already be installed.

## Commands

Run these from the repository root.

| Task | Command |
| --- | --- |
| Install | `npm ci` |
| Develop (server on 2593, web on 5393, both from source) | `npm run dev` |
| Typecheck every workspace | `npm run typecheck` |
| Unit tests | `npm run test:unit` |
| Integration tests (a real server in-process, headless clients) | `npm run test:integration` |
| Build every package and the web client, then smoke-test the built server | `npm run build` |
| Convert the Tiled map `assets/source/maps/slice-sea.tmj` into `packages/content/src/maps/sliceSea.ts` (validated first) | `npm run map:build` |
| Check that the generated map module is up to date (a unit test checks this too) | `npm run map:check` |
| Run the built server | `npm start` |
| Browser tests (start the server and web client themselves) | `npx --no-install playwright test --config=tests/e2e/playwright.config.ts` |

Set `VITE_SERVER_URL` when the web client should reach a server other than port 2593 on its own host.

**Simulated latency for duel playtests (development only).** With `npm run dev`, add `?rtt=80&jitter=20` to the page's address (for example `http://localhost:5393/?rtt=80&jitter=20`) before creating or joining a duel: that browser's duel socket is then delayed by half the round trip each way, plus a uniform ±jitter/2, in order, round-trip probes included, so the HUD's round trip and the chosen input delay reflect it. Give both browsers the setting to test §19's 30, 80 or 150 ms. It is read only when `import.meta.env.DEV` is true and is absent from the production build (`npm run build` checks). Loss is simulated only by the test harness (`ImpairmentProxy` in `packages/test-tools`).

The map is generated from an ASCII sketch of one quarter, `assets/source/maps/sliceSeaSketch.ts`: edit it, then run `npm run map:build -- --from-sketch` (details in `assets/source/maps/README.md`). The `.tmj` stays within the Tiled subset documented in `packages/content/src/tiledMap.ts`, so [Tiled](https://www.mapeditor.org/) opens it.

`PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_PLACEMENT=1` is a test-only setting, off by default: the dev and test entry point (`npm run dev`, and the server the browser tests start) then serves `POST /__test/place`, which puts a player's ship on a chosen tile. The others, also off by default: `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_SOURCES` (for example `sun,sun|moon,moon|sun,sun|moon,moon`, each island's source types) deals every match's rubbings in that layout instead of the private shuffle; `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_TIMINGS` (for example `ready=60,protection=20,match=12000`, in world ticks, 20 a second; also `sectors` and `carriers` for the two timed reveals) shortens the countdown, the departure protection, the match and its reveals; `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_FINAL_SECRET` (a 32-bit number) pins the secret that picks the final island. `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_RUINS` (for example `*=open`, or `0=sequence,1=navigation`) opens islands from the start or pins an island's ruin kind. `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_FRUITS` (for example `ember,smoke,smoke,ember`) pins the fruit on each island's pedestal. With the placement setting on, `POST /__test/next-match-timings` (for example `{"match": 400}`) also gives the next match created its own timings, and `POST /__test/next-match-ruins` (for example `{"0": "sequence"}`) its own ruins. The production entry (`npm start`) reads none of them.

The integration tests run in two groups (`npm run test:integration`): most files in parallel worker processes, with test-only shorter timings, then the netcode harness files (`duelHarness`, `seaDuelHarness`, `devLatency`) one after another on a quiet machine.

## Layout

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `apps/web` | Phaser client: landing page, HUD, input, interpolated rendering, the duel arena and HUD |
| `apps/server` | Colyseus match and duel server |
| `packages/simulation` | Pure fixed-step simulation shared by client and server, the duel and its rollback netcode |
| `packages/protocol` | Message schemas, validation and the protocol version |
| `packages/content` | The generated sea map, the Tiled converter, map validators, tuning, rules presets and frame budgets |
| `packages/test-tools` | Headless match and duel clients, the network impairment proxy and the duel replay wrapper |
| `tests/unit`, `tests/integration`, `tests/e2e` | Cross-package unit tests, server integration tests, Playwright scenarios |
| `assets/source/maps` | The Tiled source map and the sketch it is generated from |
| `assets/source`, `assets/exported`, `infra` | Placeholders for art sources, runtime assets and deployment |
